  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Venda\"\nsubtitle: \"South Africa\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Venḓa, also called VhaVenḓa or Vhangona, are a Bantu people. |\n| Where | They live mainly in northern Limpopo near the South African-Zimbabwean border. |\n| How many | There were 844,000 Venda speakers in Vhembe district and 1.2 million in South Africa in the 2011 census. |\n| Language | Venḓa, or Tshivenḓa, is a Bantu language related to Shona, including Karanga and Kalanga. |\n| Religion | The profile does not describe current Venda religion or named spiritual practices. |\n| Known for | Mapungubwe · D’zata royal kraal · Musangwe fist-fighting · Drums and xylophones · Metalwork and pottery |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Venḓa are a Bantu people of Southern Africa, centered in northern Limpopo. Their history is linked with Mapungubwe, Great Zimbabwe, the Soutpansberg and the Limpopo River. Museum records show pottery, gourds, metalwork, wooden tools, musical instruments, clothing, jewelry and domestic life. The profile also documents musangwe, a traditional bare-knuckle fist-fighting practice.\n\n## Material culture\n\nVenda material culture includes decorated pottery and gourds, copper and iron objects, wooden tools, drums, xylophones, beadwork and household vessels.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nMuseum photographs show girls wearing aprons and a girl wearing a striped wrapper.\n\n- **Striped wrapper** — A museum photograph shows a girl wearing a striped wrapper while carrying a basket on her head.\n- **Aprons** — Several photographs show dancing girls wearing aprons, including a group lying on the ground.\n\n### Architecture\n\nVenda settlement history is linked with royal kraals and decorated compounds.\n\n- **D’zata royal kraal** (*D’zata*) — The legendary chief Thoho-ya-Ndou is said to have founded a royal kraal called D’zata, whose remains are a National Monument.\n- **Decorated walled compound** — A photograph shows women and children inside a decorated walled compound beside a thatched building at Tshakhuma.\n- **Circular wall** — Another photograph shows a woman and two children beside a low circular wall containing a palm tree.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nThe profile records decorated pottery and gourds, copper and iron objects, and wooden household tools.\n\n- **Decorated earthenware bowls** — The catalogue records earthenware bowls decorated with red and burnished graphite.\n- **Gourd porridge bowl** — The catalogue records gourd porridge bowls, including one with burnt ornament.\n- **Poker-work gourd vessel** — A gourd vessel is decorated with figures outlined in poker work and marked “MAKILIEBIN.”\n- **Copper currency bar** (*lerale*) — A cylindrical copper currency bar was called lerale.\n- **Composite weapons** — The catalogue records composite axes and composite spears.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nThe catalogue records bracelets, wristlets, charms and an ostrich-eggshell head ornament.\n\n- **Bronze wire and hair bracelet** — One bracelet is made from bronze wire and animal hair.\n- **Beaded hair wristlet** — A composite wristlet uses animal hairs with two groups of beads attached.\n- **Iron charms on bead string** — Iron charms are attached to a fibre string of glass beads.\n- **Ostrich-eggshell head ornament** — A head ornament consists of one row of ostrich-eggshell beads on a fibre string.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nVenda musical instruments in the catalogue include several drums and a gourd-resonator xylophone.\n\n- **Composite drum** — One composite drum has a wooden body, a hide membrane and a loop handle.\n- **Handled wooden drum** — Another drum has a wooden body, a hide membrane and a handle.\n- **Four-handled drum** — A wood and hide drum has a solid wooden resonator, four handles, a base hole and a rattle inside.\n- **Gourd-resonator xylophone** — A composite xylophone has wooden slats joined by strings and suspended over gourd resonators.\n- **Drum playing** — Photographs from Sibasa show two young girls playing drums held between their legs.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nPhotographs show lines and groups of dancing girls wearing aprons, but the dance is not named.\n\n- **Line dance** — One photograph shows a line of dancing girls wearing aprons.\n- **Long dance line** — Another photograph shows a long line of dancing girls.\n- **Ground dance pose** — A photograph shows a group of girls wearing aprons lying on the ground.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nThe profile documents musangwe, a supervised bare-knuckle fight used for entertainment and respect.\n\n- **Bare-knuckle fist fighting** (*musangwe*) — Musangwe is a traditional Venda bare-knuckle practice that offers entertainment and a way to earn respect.\n- **Village-supervised match** — Village elders supervise musangwe, forbid biting and kicking, and punish rule breaking.\n- **Chief’s prize** (*Khosi; Vhamusanda*) — Victors receive prizes chosen by the Khosi, meaning chief, or Vhamusanda, meaning headman.\n\n## Foodways\n\nThe profile records millet, sorghum, livestock, cooking over a fire and several food vessels.\n\n- **Millet and sorghum farming** — Mapungubwe records show people growing millet and sorghum.\n- **Livestock keeping** — People at Mapungubwe tended cattle, goats and sheep.\n- **Fire cooking** — A photograph shows a woman preparing food in a cooking pot over a fire at Sibasa.\n- **Gourd porridge spoon** — The catalogue records a wooden porridge spoon and a ladle alongside gourd porridge bowls.\n- **Head-carried basket** — A photograph shows a girl carrying a basket on her head beside pots, calabashes and a mortar.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nOral tradition names Shiriyadenga as the first king of Venda and Mapungubwe.\n\n- **King Shiriyadenga** (*Shiriyadenga*) — Oral tradition holds Shiriyadenga to have been the first king of Venda and Mapungubwe.\n- **Kingdom of Mapungubwe** (*Mapungubwe*) — Mapungubwe was a trade center from about 1220 to 1300 AD and later lost power as people moved toward Great Zimbabwe.\n- **Head of the Elephant** (*Thoho-ya-Ndou*) — Thoho-ya-Ndou is a legendary chief whose name is translated as “Head of the Elephant.”\n\n## Language & religion\n\nVenḓa is an official Bantu language of South Africa and Zimbabwe, but the profile does not describe current religion.\n\n- **Venḓa language** (*Venḓa; Tshivenḓa*) — Venḓa, or Tshivenḓa, is mainly spoken by the Venda people in northern Limpopo and by some Lemba people in South Africa.\n- **Sintu writing system** (*Isibheqe Sohlamvu; Ditema tsa Dinoko*) — Venda uses the sintu writing system called Isibheqe Sohlamvu or Ditema tsa Dinoko.\n- **Luṱhofunḓeraru lwa Mibvumo** — Luṱhofunḓeraru lwa Mibvumo is the technical Venda name for this writing system.\n- **Additional accented letters** (*ḓ; ḽ; ṋ; ṱ; ṅ*) — The Latin alphabet used for Venḓa adds the accented letters ḓ, ḽ, ṋ, ṱ and ṅ.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Venḓa* — Venda language or people\n- *VhaVenḓa* — Venda people\n- *Vhangona* — Another name for the Venda\n- *Tshivenḓa* — Venda language\n- *D’zata* — Royal kraal\n- *lerale* — Copper currency bar\n- *musangwe* — Traditional bare-knuckle fighting\n- *Khosi* — Chief\n- *Vhamusanda* — Headman\n- *Shiriyadenga* — First king in oral tradition\n- *Mapungubwe* — Historic kingdom and trade center\n- *Thoho-ya-Ndou* — Head of the Elephant\n- *Isibheqe Sohlamvu* — Sintu writing system\n- *Ditema tsa Dinoko* — Sintu writing system\n- *Luṱhofunḓeraru lwa Mibvumo* — Technical Venda name for the writing system\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- George Poulos, *A Linguistic Analysis of Venda*, Via Afrika, 1990.\n- The Venda language material cites Zach Gershkoff’s *Tshivenḓa Grammar Guide*, US Peace Corps, 2012.\n- Wikipedia, [Venda people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venda_people).\n- Wikipedia, [Venda language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venda_language).\n- British Museum collection records for the axes, spears, bowls, divination objects, musical instruments, adornment and photographs described above.\n",
